Bitmask DP over which elements have been used, with at most 14 elements (so 2^14 masks).

1. Precompute `g[i][j] = gcd(nums[i], nums[j])` for every pair.
2. Let `dp[mask]` be the best score after removing exactly the elements in `mask`. Only masks with an even popcount are reachable, since elements are removed in pairs.
3. For a reachable mask, the next operation index is `op = popcount(mask) / 2 + 1`.
4. To avoid counting the same pair choice twice, always pair the lowest unused index `i` with some other unused `j`. The transition is:
   `dp[mask | bit_i | bit_j] = max(..., dp[mask] + op * g[i][j])`.
5. The answer is `dp[full]`, where `full` has all bits set.

Initialise `dp` to -1 to mark unreachable states, and skip those masks.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number}
 */
var maxScore = function(nums) {
    const m = nums.length;
    const gcd = (a, b) => { while (b) { [a, b] = [b, a % b]; } return a; };
    const g = Array.from({length: m}, () => new Array(m).fill(0));
    for (let i = 0; i < m; i++)
        for (let j = i + 1; j < m; j++)
            g[i][j] = g[j][i] = gcd(nums[i], nums[j]);
    const full = (1 << m) - 1;
    const dp = new Array(1 << m).fill(-1);
    dp[0] = 0;
    for (let mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
        if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
        let cnt = 0, t = mask;
        while (t) { cnt += t & 1; t >>= 1; }
        if (cnt & 1) continue;
        const op = cnt / 2 + 1;
        let i = 0;
        while (mask & (1 << i)) i++;
        for (let j = i + 1; j < m; j++) {
            if (mask & (1 << j)) continue;
            const nm = mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j);
            const v = dp[mask] + op * g[i][j];
            if (v > dp[nm]) dp[nm] = v;
        }
    }
    return dp[full];
};
```

