inference_trace:
Sort the array so every possible child of a value appears before it. Let `dp[v]` be the number of trees whose root is `v`.

- Each value can stand alone as a leaf, so `dp[v]` starts at 1.
- For each earlier value `a` that divides `v`, let `b = v / a`. If `b` is in the array, add `dp[a] * dp[b]` to `dp[v]`.
- The loop visits ordered pairs `(a, b)`, which counts left/right swaps as different trees, matching Example 2.

The answer is the sum of all `dp` values, taken modulo 1e9+7.

**Overflow:** `dp` values are below about 1e9, so their product can reach about 1e18. That exceeds 2^53 and loses precision with plain numbers, so the multiplication uses BigInt.

**Performance:** BigInt work is only done for matching divisor pairs. Including the modulo inside the multiplication, 1e6 such operations is fine.

**Speedup:** the loop over `j` can stop once `sorted[j]^2 > v`. That would need the pair counted once when `a == b` and twice otherwise. The plain loop over all `j < i` with a divisibility check is about 5e5 checks, which is fast enough, so the simple version is used.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} arr
 * @return {number}
 */
var numFactoredBinaryTrees = function(arr) {
    const MOD = 1000000007n;
    const a = arr.slice().sort((x, y) => x - y);
    const n = a.length;
    const idx = new Map();
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) idx.set(a[i], i);
    const dp = new Array(n).fill(1n);
    let total = 0n;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        const v = a[i];
        let cur = 1n;
        for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            const x = a[j];
            if (x * x > v) break;
            if (v % x === 0) {
                const y = v / x;
                if (idx.has(y)) {
                    const k = idx.get(y);
                    let p = dp[j] * dp[k];
                    if (x !== y) p *= 2n;
                    cur += p;
                }
            }
        }
        dp[i] = cur % MOD;
        total = (total + dp[i]) % MOD;
    }
    return Number(total);
};
```

