Take the first two points as the reference line. For every other point, check that it is collinear with them using the cross product instead of slopes, which avoids division and vertical-line edge cases:

(y − y0)·(x1 − x0) must equal (x − x0)·(y1 − y0).

If any point breaks this equality, the points are not on one line. With coordinates up to 10^4, each product is at most about 4×10^8, so there is no overflow risk.

