Simulate with a stack, cycling the operators `*`, `/`, `+`, `-` from the second number onward:

- **`*`**: multiply the top of the stack by the current number.
- **`/`**: divide the top by the current number, truncating toward zero. Truncation is the right behaviour, because the subtracted group is pushed as a negative value. For example, `-(6*5)/4` should give `-7`, which is `-(30/4)` truncated.
- **`+`**: push the current number.
- **`-`**: push the negated current number.

The answer is the sum of the stack.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number} n
 * @return {number}
 */
var clumsy = function(n) {
    const stack = [n];
    let op = 0;
    for (let i = n - 1; i >= 1; i--) {
        if (op === 0) {
            stack.push(stack.pop() * i);
        } else if (op === 1) {
            stack.push(Math.trunc(stack.pop() / i));
        } else if (op === 2) {
            stack.push(i);
        } else {
            stack.push(-i);
        }
        op = (op + 1) % 4;
    }
    let sum = 0;
    for (const v of stack) sum += v;
    return sum;
