The maximum OR of any subset is the OR of all elements, since adding elements never clears bits.

So I count subsets whose OR equals that value with a recursive DFS. At each index, the element is either included or skipped. When all indices are processed, the subset counts if its accumulated OR equals the target.

The empty subset has OR 0. Since every `nums[i] >= 1`, the target is at least 1, so the empty subset is never counted.

With `n <= 16`, there are at most 2^16 = 65,536 subsets, so full enumeration is fast.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number}
 */
var countMaxOrSubsets = function(nums) {
    let target = 0;
    for (const x of nums) target |= x;
    const n = nums.length;
    const dfs = (i, cur) => {
        if (i === n) return cur === target ? 1 : 0;
        return dfs(i + 1, cur | nums[i]) + dfs(i + 1, cur);
    };
    return dfs(0, 0);
};
```

