Two strings are close exactly when all three of these hold:

1. **Same length.** Neither operation changes the length of a string.
2. **Same set of characters.** Operation 2 only swaps between characters that already exist, so no new letter can appear and no existing letter can disappear.
3. **Same multiset of frequencies.** Operation 1 lets us rearrange characters freely. Operation 2 swaps the counts of any two existing letters. Together they allow any permutation of the counts among the present letters.

Approach:
- Count the occurrences of each of the 26 letters in both words.
- For each letter, check that it is either present in both words or absent from both.
- Sort both count arrays and compare them element by element.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {string} word1
 * @param {string} word2
 * @return {boolean}
 */
var closeStrings = function(word1, word2) {
    if (word1.length !== word2.length) return false;
    const a = new Array(26).fill(0), b = new Array(26).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < word1.length; i++) a[word1.charCodeAt(i) - 97]++;
    for (let i = 0; i < word2.length; i++) b[word2.charCodeAt(i) - 97]++;
    for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
        if ((a[i] === 0) !== (b[i] === 0)) return false;
    }
    a.sort((x, y) => x - y);
    b.sort((x, y) => x - y);
    for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) if (a[i] !== b[i]) return false;
    return true;
};
```

