Use patience sorting in O(n log n). Keep an array `tails`, where `tails[k]` is the smallest possible tail value of any increasing subsequence of length `k+1`.

For each number `x`:
1. Binary-search for the first index whose tail is `>= x` (lower bound). Using `>=` keeps the subsequence strictly increasing, since an equal value replaces rather than extends.
2. If that index is past the end, append `x`, which extends the longest subsequence by one.
3. Otherwise, replace the tail at that index with `x`.

The length of `tails` at the end is the answer.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number}
 */
var lengthOfLIS = function(nums) {
    const tails = [];
    for (const x of nums) {
        let lo = 0, hi = tails.length;
        while (lo < hi) {
            const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
            if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;
            else hi = mid;
        }
        tails[lo] = x;
    }
    return tails.length;
};
```

