Use a bitmask DP over "slot positions". Treat each slot as two separate positions, giving `2 * numSlots` positions (at most 18). Position `j` belongs to slot `(j >> 1) + 1`.

- Define `dp[mask]` as the best AND sum after placing the first `popcount(mask)` numbers into exactly the positions set in `mask`.
- Let `i = popcount(mask)`. If `i < n`, the next number to place is `nums[i]`.
- For every free position `j` (bit `j` not set in `mask`), update `dp[mask | (1 << j)]` with `dp[mask] + (nums[i] & slot)`.
- Masks whose popcount exceeds `n` are skipped, since they never apply a transition.
- The answer is the maximum `dp[mask]` over all masks with popcount equal to `n`.

Complexity: there are 2^18 states, about 262k, each trying 18 positions. That is roughly 4.7M operations, which is fast.

Unreachable states are initialized to -1, so only reachable ones (`dp >= 0`) are expanded. Popcounts are computed incrementally with `pc[m] = pc[m >> 1] + (m & 1)`.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @param {number} numSlots
 * @return {number}
 */
var maximumANDSum = function(nums, numSlots) {
    const n = nums.length;
    const m = 2 * numSlots;
    const size = 1 << m;
    const dp = new Int32Array(size).fill(-1);
    const pc = new Uint8Array(size);
    for (let i = 1; i < size; i++) pc[i] = pc[i >> 1] + (i & 1);
