Track two maps while scanning prefixes:
- `cnt[x]`: how many times value `x` has appeared so far.
- `freq[f]`: how many distinct values currently appear exactly `f` times.

Also keep `maxF`, the largest count of any value. For a prefix of length `len = i + 1`, removing one element works in three cases:

1. **Every value appears once** (`maxF == 1`). Remove any element.
2. **One value appears `maxF` times, all others appear `maxF - 1` times.** This holds when `freq[maxF] == 1` and `maxF + freq[maxF-1] * (maxF-1) == len`. Remove one copy of the most frequent value.
3. **One value appears exactly once, all others appear `maxF` times.** This holds when `freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len`. Remove that single occurrence.

Case 2 also covers a prefix containing only one distinct value: `freq[maxF] == 1` and `freq[maxF-1] == 0`, so the check reduces to `maxF == len`.

Whenever any case holds, record `len` as the answer. Typed arrays give O(1) updates, so the whole scan is O(n).

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number}
 */
var maxEqualFreq = function(nums) {
    const n = nums.length;
    const cnt = new Int32Array(100002);
    const freq = new Int32Array(n + 2);
    let maxF = 0, ans = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        const x = nums[i];
        const c = cnt[x];
        if (c > 0) freq[c]--;
        cnt[x] = c + 1;
        freq[c + 1]++;
        if (c + 1 > maxF) maxF = c + 1;
        const len = i + 1;
        if (maxF === 1 ||
            (freq[maxF] === 1 && maxF + freq[maxF - 1] * (maxF - 1) === len) ||
            (freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 === len)) {
            ans = len;
        }
    }
    return ans;
};
```

