Approach: use prefix sums so any window sum is a constant-time lookup, then make one linear pass for each ordering (first-length window before the second, and the reverse).

Let `P` be the prefix sum array, so `P[i]` is the sum of `nums[0..i-1]`.

To handle "a window of length `L` placed before a window of length `M`":
- Walk `i` from `L+M` up to `n`.
- At each step, update the best `L`-window that ends at or before `i-M`. Its sum is `P[i-M] - P[i-M-L]`, and we keep a running maximum `maxL`.
- Then try `maxL` plus the `M`-window ending at `i`, whose sum is `P[i] - P[i-M]`.

The answer is the larger of `f(firstLen, secondLen)` and `f(secondLen, firstLen)`, which covers both orderings. Total time is O(n).

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @param {number} firstLen
 * @param {number} secondLen
 * @return {number}
 */
var maxSumTwoNoOverlap = function(nums, firstLen, secondLen) {
    const n = nums.length;
    const P = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + nums[i];
    const f = (L, M) => {
        let maxL = 0, res = 0;
        for (let i = L + M; i <= n; i++) {
            maxL = Math.max(maxL, P[i - M] - P[i - M - L]);
            res = Math.max(res, maxL + P[i] - P[i - M]);
        }
        return res;
    };
    return Math.max(f(firstLen, secondLen), f(secondLen, firstLen));
};
```

