inference_trace:
Use memoised recursion. Eating one orange at a time is only useful to reach a multiple of 2 or 3, so from `n` there are two candidate moves:

- Spend `n % 2` days eating single oranges, then halve: `1 + n % 2 + f(floor(n/2))`.
- Spend `n % 3` days eating single oranges, then cut to a third: `1 + n % 3 + f(floor(n/3))`.

Take the minimum of the two. The base cases are `f(0) = 0` and `f(1) = 1`.

The only reachable states are values of the form `floor(n / (2^a * 3^b))`, so a memo map keeps the number of states small (logarithmic squared in `n`).

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number} n
 * @return {number}
 */
var minDays = function(n) {
    const memo = new Map();
    const f = (x) => {
        if (x <= 1) return x;
        if (memo.has(x)) return memo.get(x);
        const r = 1 + Math.min(x % 2 + f(Math.floor(x / 2)), x % 3 + f(Math.floor(x / 3)));
        memo.set(x, r);
        return r;
    };
    return f(n);
};
```

