Sort the array in descending order and take elements from the front, keeping a running sum. Stop as soon as the taken sum is strictly greater than the remaining sum (total minus taken).

Why this is correct:
- Taking the largest available elements gives the biggest possible sum for any given count, so it reaches the threshold with the fewest elements.
- For that minimum count, the largest elements also give the maximum total sum, which settles the tie-break.
- The taken elements are already in non-increasing order, so no extra sort is needed for the output.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number[]}
 */
var minSubsequence = function(nums) {
    const sorted = [...nums].sort((a, b) => b - a);
    const total = sorted.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
    const res = [];
    let sum = 0;
    for (const x of sorted) {
        sum += x;
        res.push(x);
        if (sum > total - sum) break;
    }
    return res;
};
```

