inference_trace:
Use a monotonic stack and loop over the array twice (indices `0` to `2n-1`) to simulate the circular wrap.

- The stack holds indices whose next greater element hasn't been found yet. Their values are non-increasing from bottom to top.
- At each step, look at the current value `nums[i % n]`. While it is greater than the value at the index on top of the stack, pop that index and set its answer to the current value.
- During the first pass (`i < n`), push the current index onto the stack.
- The second pass only resolves elements whose next greater number lies after a wrap-around.
- Any index still on the stack at the end keeps its default answer of `-1`.

Each index is pushed and popped at most once, so this runs in O(n) time and O(n) space.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number[]}
 */
var nextGreaterElements = function(nums) {
    const n = nums.length;
    const res = new Array(n).fill(-1);
    const stack = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
        const v = nums[i % n];
        while (stack.length && nums[stack[stack.length - 1]] < v) {
            res[stack.pop()] = v;
        }
        if (i < n) stack.push(i);
    }
    return res;
};
```

