Use a bitmask DP over subsets of `nums`.

1. Let `total` be the sum of `nums`. If `total` is not divisible by `k`, return `false`. Otherwise the target sum per subset is `target = total / k`.
2. Sort `nums`. If the largest element exceeds `target`, return `false`.
3. Let `dp[mask]` be the running sum of the current (partially filled) bucket after using the elements in `mask`, taken modulo `target`. Use `-1` for unreachable masks, and set `dp[0] = 0`.
4. For each reachable mask, try each unused element `i`:
   - Skip it if `dp[mask] + nums[i] > target`. Because the array is sorted, every later element is also too big, so break out of the loop.
   - Otherwise set `dp[mask | (1 << i)] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % target`.
5. The answer is `true` exactly when `dp[full] === 0`, where `full` is the mask with all elements used.

Since all numbers are positive, a reachable full mask always has `dp[full] = 0`. In other words, reaching the full mask means the elements were packed into complete buckets of size `target`.

This runs in O(2^n · n) time with n ≤ 16, which is fine.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @param {number} k
 * @return {boolean}
 */
var canPartitionKSubsets = function(nums, k) {
    const n = nums.length;
    let total = 0;
    for (const x of nums) total += x;
    if (total % k !== 0) return false;
    const target = total / k;
    nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    if (nums[n - 1] > target) return false;
    const size = 1 << n;
    const dp = new Int32Array(size).fill(-1);
    dp[0] = 0;
    for (let mask = 0; mask < size; mask++) {
        if (dp[mask] === -1) continue;
        const cur = dp[mask];
        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (mask & (1 << i)) continue;
            if (cur + nums[i] > target) break;
            const nm = mask | (1 << i);
            if (dp[nm] === -1) {
                dp[nm] = (cur + nums[i]) % target;
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[size - 1] === 0;
};
```

