Treat the grid as one flat array of length `total = m * n`. A shift moves every element one position forward in that flat order, wrapping from the end back to the start. After `k` shifts, the element at flat index `idx` lands at `(idx + k) % total`.

For each cell `(i, j)`:
1. Compute its flat index `idx = i * n + j`.
2. Compute the new index `ni = (idx + k) % total`.
3. Place the value at row `floor(ni / n)`, column `ni % n` in a new grid.

This runs in O(m·n) time and works for any `k`, including `k` larger than `total`.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[][]} grid
 * @param {number} k
 * @return {number[][]}
 */
var shiftGrid = function(grid, k) {
    const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length, total = m * n;
    const res = Array.from({length: m}, () => new Array(n));
    for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            const idx = (i * n + j + k) % total;
            res[Math.floor(idx / n)][idx % n] = grid[i][j];
        }
    }
    return res;
};
```

