Use the greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the most recent `'*'`:

1. Keep pointer `i` in `s` and `j` in `p`.
2. If `p[j]` is `'?'` or equals `s[i]`, advance both pointers.
3. If `p[j]` is `'*'`, record its position as `star`, record `i` as `match`, and advance `j`. The star tentatively matches the empty sequence.
4. Otherwise, if a star was seen earlier, backtrack: set `j = star + 1`, increment `match`, and set `i = match`. This lets the star absorb one more character.
5. If none of these apply, return `false`.
6. Once `s` is exhausted, skip any trailing `'*'` in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` reaches the end of `p`.

This runs in O(n·m) time in the worst case (typically much faster) and uses O(1) extra space.

```javascript
/**
 * @param {string} s
 * @param {string} p
 * @return {boolean}
 */
var isMatch = function(s, p) {
    let i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, match = 0;
    const n = s.length, m = p.length;
    while (i < n) {
        if (j < m && (p[j] === '?' || p[j] === s[i])) {
            i++; j++;
        } else if (j < m && p[j] === '*') {
            star = j;
            match = i;
            j++;
        } else if (star !== -1) {
            j = star + 1;
            match++;
            i = match;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
    while (j < m && p[j] === '*') j++;
    return j === m;
};
```

